/* Review questions and AP exam links, merged into each tool by BioLab.addReview. "verify" is read only by the test script. */
BioLab.addReview({
stoich:{
  apNote:'Released questions from College Board. Heat and yield questions on the exam often depend on finding the limiting reactant first.',
  ap:[
    {title:'2026 AP Chemistry free-response, Question 7, part B',url:'https://apcentral.collegeboard.org/media/pdf/ap26-frq-chemistry.pdf',note:'Find the heat released by a mix of two reactants. The question doesn’t say “limiting reactant,” but you need it.'},
    {title:'2025 AP Chemistry free-response, Question 3, part D',url:'https://apcentral.collegeboard.org/media/pdf/ap25-frq-chemistry.pdf',note:'Some of the limiting reactant is lost before the reaction. What happens to the result?'},
    {title:'AP Chemistry exam page',url:'https://apcentral.collegeboard.org/courses/ap-chemistry/exam',note:'Exam format and past questions from College Board.'}
  ],
  questions:[
  {id:'smores',kind:'real',lvl:'bio',
    q:'One s’more takes 2 graham crackers, 1 marshmallow and 3 chocolate pieces. You have 10 crackers, 3 marshmallows and 15 chocolate pieces. How many s’mores can you make?',
    choices:['3','5','10','28'],correct:0,
    steps:['Find how many batches each ingredient allows: crackers 10 ÷ 2 = 5, marshmallows 3 ÷ 1 = 3, chocolate 15 ÷ 3 = 5.','The smallest number sets the limit, so you can make 3. Marshmallows are the limiting reactant.','You’ll have 4 crackers and 6 chocolate pieces left over. A chemist would call those the excess.'],
    lab:{set:{rx:'smores',units:'count',aN:10,bN:3,cN:15},look:'Watch the batches form. Read <b>Product made</b> and what’s left of each ingredient.'},verify:[['p1',3,.01],['r1',4,.01],['r2',0,.01],['r3',6,.01]]},
  {id:'stove',kind:'real',lvl:'bio',
    q:'A gas stove burns methane: CH₄ + 2O₂ → CO₂ + 2H₂O. Suppose 5 CH₄ molecules meet 6 O₂ molecules. What happens?',
    choices:['All the methane burns, because there are fewer methane molecules.','3 CH₄ burn and 2 are left, because oxygen runs out first.','5 CH₄ burn, and 1 O₂ is left.','Nothing burns unless the numbers match exactly.'],correct:1,
    steps:['Each CH₄ needs 2 O₂. So 6 O₂ is only enough for 6 ÷ 2 = 3 CH₄.','O₂ is limiting, even though there are more O₂ molecules than CH₄ molecules. That’s the mistake to avoid: the limiting reactant isn’t always the smaller number.','This is why a burner needs a good air supply. In a real flame, too little oxygen also makes carbon monoxide, which this model leaves out.'],
    lab:{set:{rx:'ch4',units:'count',aN:5,bN:6},look:'Watch which reactant runs out, then read what’s left of each.'},verify:[['r1',2,.01],['r2',0,.01]]},
  {id:'ammonia',kind:'test',lvl:'bio',
    q:'N₂ + 3H₂ → 2NH₃. You start with 4 N₂ and 9 H₂. How many NH₃ can form?',
    choices:['4','6','8','13'],correct:1,
    steps:['Batches allowed: N₂ gives 4 ÷ 1 = 4, and H₂ gives 9 ÷ 3 = 3. H₂ is limiting, even though there’s more of it.','Each batch makes 2 NH₃, so 3 batches make 6 NH₃.','One N₂ is left over. Check by counting atoms: 8 N and 18 H before, and 6 NH₃ plus 1 N₂ after, which is also 8 N and 18 H.'],
    lab:{set:{rx:'nh3',units:'count',aN:4,bN:9},look:'Read <b>Product made</b> and what’s left of each reactant.'},verify:[['p1',6,.01],['r1',1,.01],['r2',0,.01]]},
  {id:'rocket',kind:'real',lvl:'honors',
    q:'The Space Shuttle’s main engines burned hydrogen and oxygen (2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O), fed about 6 g of O₂ for every 1 g of H₂. Which runs out first?',
    choices:['H₂, because there’s far less of it by mass','O₂, because 6 g of O₂ is fewer moles than the 1 g of H₂ needs','Neither, because 6:1 is the exact recipe','You can’t tell without the total mass.'],correct:1,
    steps:['Convert to moles: 1 g H₂ ÷ 2.016 g/mol = 0.50 mol. 6 g O₂ ÷ 32.00 g/mol = 0.19 mol.','0.50 mol H₂ would need 0.25 mol O₂, and only 0.19 mol is there. O₂ is limiting.','The exact recipe is about 8 g O₂ per 1 g H₂. Engines like this run with extra hydrogen on purpose. Compare moles, never grams.'],
    lab:{set:{rx:'water',units:'g',aG:5,bG:30},look:'Same 6-to-1 ratio, scaled up to 5 g and 30 g. Read <b>Limiting reactant</b> and how much hydrogen is left.'},verify:[['lim','Oxygen|O2|O₂'],['theo',33.8,.2]]},
  {id:'grill',kind:'real',lvl:'honors',
    q:'A propane grill has a blocked air vent, so 1.0 mol C₃H₈ gets only 3.0 mol O₂. If it all burned by C₃H₈ + 5O₂ → 3CO₂ + 4H₂O, how much propane would be left?',
    num:{ans:.4,tol:.02,unit:'mol'},
    steps:['1.0 mol propane would need 5.0 mol O₂, and only 3.0 mol is there. O₂ is limiting.','3.0 mol O₂ burns 3.0 ÷ 5 = 0.60 mol propane.','Left over: 1.0 − 0.60 = 0.40 mol. A real starved flame also makes soot and carbon monoxide, which is why grills stay outdoors.'],
    lab:{set:{rx:'c3h8',units:'mol',aM:1,bM:3},look:'Read what’s left of each reactant and which one is limiting.'},verify:[['r1',.4,.01],['r2',0,.01]]},
  {id:'copper',kind:'test',lvl:'honors',
    q:'2Al + 3CuCl₂ → 2AlCl₃ + 3Cu. You mix 13.5 g Al with 40.0 g CuCl₂. What mass of copper can form? (Al 26.98, CuCl₂ 134.45, Cu 63.55 g/mol)',
    num:{ans:18.9,tol:.4,unit:'g'},
    steps:['Convert to moles: 13.5 ÷ 26.98 = 0.500 mol Al. 40.0 ÷ 134.45 = 0.298 mol CuCl₂.','0.500 mol Al would need 0.750 mol CuCl₂, and only 0.298 mol is there. CuCl₂ is limiting.','CuCl₂ and Cu are 3 to 3, so 0.298 mol Cu forms: 0.298 × 63.55 = 18.9 g.'],
    lab:{set:{rx:'al',units:'g',aG:13.5,bG:40,loss:0},look:'Read <b>Limiting reactant</b> and <b>Theoretical yield</b>.'},verify:[['lim','CuCl|copper'],['theo',18.9,.1]]},
  {id:'wet',kind:'test',lvl:'honors',
    q:'A student calculates a percent yield of 125%. What is the most likely reason?',
    choices:['The reaction made more product than the limiting reactant allows.','The product was weighed before it was dry.','Some product was spilled while collecting it.','The student used too much of the excess reactant.'],correct:1,
    steps:['Atoms are conserved, so a reaction can’t make more product than the limiting reactant allows.','A yield over 100% means the weighed mass includes something extra, usually water or leftover solution.','A spill would lower the yield. Extra excess reactant wouldn’t change the theoretical yield at all.'],
    lab:{set:{rx:'al',units:'g',aG:13.5,bG:40,loss:0,wet:true},look:'The product here is weighed before it’s dry. Read <b>Percent yield</b>.'},verify:[['pyield',125,.5]]},
  {id:'heat',kind:'ap',lvl:'ap',
    q:'CH₄ + 2O₂ → CO₂ + 2H₂O, with ΔH° = −890 kJ per mole of reaction as written. A mix of 8.0 g CH₄ and 16.0 g O₂ reacts completely. How much heat is released? (CH₄ 16.04, O₂ 32.00 g/mol)',
    num:{ans:222.5,tol:3,unit:'kJ'},
    steps:['Moles: 8.0 ÷ 16.04 = 0.50 mol CH₄. 16.0 ÷ 32.00 = 0.50 mol O₂.','Divide by coefficients: CH₄ gives 0.50 ÷ 1 = 0.50, and O₂ gives 0.50 ÷ 2 = 0.25. O₂ is limiting, so the reaction runs 0.25 mol.','Heat released = 0.25 mol × 890 kJ/mol = 222.5 kJ. Using 0.50 mol would give twice the right answer, which is the trap in this kind of question.'],
    lab:{set:{rx:'ch4',units:'g',aG:8,bG:16},look:'The tool doesn’t show heat, but it does show that O₂ is limiting. <b>Theoretical yield</b> of CO₂ is 0.25 mol × 44.01 g/mol, about 11 g.'},verify:[['lim','O₂|O2|Oxygen|oxygen'],['theo',11.0,.1]]},
  {id:'stuck',kind:'ap',lvl:'ap',
    q:'In 2Al + 3CuCl₂ → 2AlCl₃ + 3Cu, Al is limiting. A student records 5.0 g Al, but 1.0 g stays stuck to the weighing paper. How does this affect the calculated percent yield of copper?',
    choices:['It’s too low: the theoretical yield is calculated from 5.0 g, but only 4.0 g reacted.','It’s too high: less Al means less product is expected.','It doesn’t change, because Al is limiting.','It doesn’t change, because CuCl₂ is in excess.'],correct:0,
    steps:['Because Al is limiting, less Al means less copper actually forms.','The student still calculates the theoretical yield from the recorded 5.0 g, so that number is too big.','Actual ÷ too-big theoretical gives a percent yield that’s too low. Say what changed, which way, and why. That chain is what scoring guides look for.'],
    lab:{set:{rx:'al',units:'g',aG:4,bG:40,loss:0},look:'This is what really reacts: 4 g of Al. Note the <b>Theoretical yield</b>, then set Al to 5 g to see what the student would calculate.'},verify:[['lim','Al|luminum'],['theo',14.1,.1]]}
  ]}
});

/* Why questions for the Unit 3 reaction tools (Nov 13, 2026): Chemistry tests on reactions Nov 17-19. */
BioLab.addReview({
balance:{questions:[
  {id:'subscript',kind:'test',lvl:'bio',
    q:'H₂ + O₂ → H₂O has 2 O on the left and 1 on the right. Why can’t you fix it by writing H₂O₂ on the right?',
    choices:['Subscripts can only change in ionic compounds','It would make the products too heavy','Changing a subscript makes a different substance: H₂O₂ is hydrogen peroxide, not water','Oxygen must always appear in pairs'],correct:2,
    steps:['A subscript is part of a substance’s identity. H₂O is water; H₂O₂ is hydrogen peroxide, a bleach.','So you may only change coefficients, the counts of whole molecules.','2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O balances: 4 H and 2 O on each side, and the product is still water.']},
  {id:'conserve',kind:'test',lvl:'bio',
    q:'Why must every element have the same number of atoms on both sides of an equation?',
    choices:['Because reactions only rearrange atoms: none are created or destroyed','Because chemists agreed to write them that way','Because products are always heavier','Because the coefficients must add to the same number'],correct:0,
    steps:['In a reaction, bonds break and new bonds form, but the atoms themselves stay.','So every atom on the left must show up on the right. That is the law of conservation of mass.','The total of the coefficients does not have to match: 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O has 3 molecules in and 2 out.']},
  {id:'coeff',kind:'test',lvl:'bio',
    q:'In 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O, what does the 2 in front of H₂O tell you?',
    choices:['Each water molecule has 2 oxygen atoms','The water is twice as hot','2 grams of water form','There are 2 water molecules: 4 H atoms and 2 O atoms in all'],correct:3,
    steps:['A coefficient counts whole molecules (or moles of them).','2H₂O means two water molecules, each with 2 H and 1 O.','So it holds 4 H and 2 O, which matches the 2H₂ and the O₂ on the left.']},
  {id:'candle',kind:'real',lvl:'bio',
    q:'A candle gets lighter as it burns. Does that break the law of conservation of mass?',
    choices:['Yes, burning destroys matter','No: the wax combines with oxygen from the air, and the products (CO₂ and water vapor) float away as gases','Yes, but only for candles','No, the candle only melts'],correct:1,
    steps:['Burning wax is a combustion reaction: wax + O₂ → CO₂ + H₂O.','The products are gases, so they leave the candle.','If you could trap the gases, the products would weigh exactly as much as the wax plus the oxygen it used.']},
  {id:'grillair',kind:'real',lvl:'bio',
    q:'Propane burns as C₃H₈ + 5O₂ → 3CO₂ + 4H₂O. Why must a gas grill be used outdoors with its vents open, never inside a garage or tent?',
    choices:['The grill gets too hot indoors','Propane only burns in sunlight','Propane needs 5 oxygen molecules for each propane molecule; with too little air it burns incompletely and makes carbon monoxide','CO₂ from the grill is poisonous in any amount'],correct:2,
    steps:['The balanced equation says each propane molecule needs 5 O₂ to burn completely to CO₂ and water.','In a closed space the oxygen runs low, so some carbon only gets one oxygen: carbon monoxide (CO).','CO has no smell and is deadly. The CDC says never to use a grill or generator inside a home, garage or tent.']},
  {id:'olast',kind:'test',lvl:'honors',
    q:'When balancing a hydrocarbon combustion such as C₃H₈ + O₂ → CO₂ + H₂O, why is it smart to balance oxygen last?',
    choices:['Oxygen is the lightest element','Oxygen appears in both products, so its count depends on the CO₂ and H₂O coefficients you set first','Oxygen never needs balancing','The rules say alphabetical order'],correct:1,
    steps:['C only appears in CO₂ and H only in H₂O, so set those first: 3CO₂ and 4H₂O.','Now count oxygen on the right: 3 × 2 + 4 × 1 = 10 O.','Then O₂ on the left needs a coefficient of 5. Fixing O first would get undone by every later change.']},
  {id:'charge',kind:'test',lvl:'ap',
    q:'Cu + Ag⁺ → Cu²⁺ + Ag has one of each atom on both sides. Why is the balanced equation Cu + 2Ag⁺ → Cu²⁺ + 2Ag?',
    choices:['Charge must balance too: Cu gives up 2 electrons and each Ag⁺ takes only 1, so 2 Ag⁺ are needed','Silver atoms come in pairs','Copper is twice as heavy as silver','The 2 is optional'],correct:0,
    steps:['Left side charge with one Ag⁺: +1. Right side: +2. Charge is not conserved, so the equation is wrong.','Electrons lost must equal electrons gained: Cu → Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻, and Ag⁺ + e⁻ → Ag.','Doubling the silver half-reaction gives Cu + 2Ag⁺ → Cu²⁺ + 2Ag: +2 on both sides.']}
]},
rxntypes:{questions:[
  {id:'decomp',kind:'test',lvl:'bio',
    q:'Why is 2H₂O₂ → 2H₂O + O₂ called a decomposition reaction?',
    choices:['Two reactants join into one product','A metal replaces another metal','It needs a flame','One reactant breaks apart into two simpler products'],correct:3,
    steps:['Look at the left side: one substance, hydrogen peroxide.','Look at the right side: two simpler substances, water and oxygen.','One thing splitting into two or more is decomposition. It is why an old bottle of peroxide loses its fizz: it slowly turns into water.']},
  {id:'evidence',kind:'test',lvl:'bio',
    q:'Bubbles, a color change, heat or a new solid count as evidence of a chemical reaction. Why doesn’t boiling water count?',
    choices:['Boiling water makes no bubbles','Steam is still H₂O: no new substance formed, so it is a physical change','Boiling is too slow','Water cannot react'],correct:1,
    steps:['A chemical reaction makes a new substance with new properties.','Boiling only moves water molecules apart. Steam, liquid water and ice are all H₂O.','So bubbles from boiling are a physical change. Bubbles from zinc in acid are hydrogen gas, a new substance.']},
  {id:'bluefade',kind:'test',lvl:'bio',
    q:'Aluminum foil sits in blue copper(II) chloride. The blue fades and red-brown copper coats the foil. Why does the blue fade?',
    choices:['The foil absorbs the blue dye','The water evaporates','Aluminum replaces copper: blue Cu²⁺ ions become copper metal, and the Al³⁺ ions that take their place are colorless','Chloride turns colorless when heated'],correct:2,
    steps:['The blue color comes from Cu²⁺ ions in the water.','Aluminum is more active, so it gives electrons to Cu²⁺: 2Al + 3CuCl₂ → 2AlCl₃ + 3Cu.','As Cu²⁺ leaves the solution as copper metal, the blue fades. This is a single-replacement reaction.']},
  {id:'bakingsoda',kind:'real',lvl:'bio',
    q:'Why do baking soda and vinegar fizz when you mix them?',
    choices:['The acid reacts with the carbonate in baking soda to make carbon dioxide gas','The vinegar boils','Baking soda dissolves and releases air','It is a physical change, like mixing salt and water'],correct:0,
    steps:['Baking soda is sodium bicarbonate, NaHCO₃. Vinegar contains acetic acid.','They swap partners (double replacement) to make carbonic acid, which breaks down into water and CO₂.','The CO₂ bubbles are the evidence of a new substance. The same gas makes pancakes and quick breads rise.']},
  {id:'ash',kind:'real',lvl:'bio',
    q:'A big log burns down to a small pile of ash. Where did most of its mass go?',
    choices:['It was destroyed by the fire','Into the ground under the fire','It turned into heat','Into the air as CO₂ and water vapor, the gases combustion makes'],correct:3,
    steps:['Burning wood is combustion: fuel + O₂ → CO₂ + H₂O.','Most of the wood is carbon, hydrogen and oxygen, and they leave as gases you can’t see.','The ash is the minerals that don’t burn. Mass is conserved; you just can’t weigh the smoke and gases easily.']},
  {id:'neutral',kind:'test',lvl:'honors',
    q:'HCl + NaOH → NaCl + H₂O is both a neutralization and a double-replacement reaction. Why both?',
    choices:['Because it gets hot','Because sodium replaces hydrogen as an element','Two compounds swap partners (double replacement), and the H⁺ and OH⁻ that pair up make water (neutralization)','Because NaCl is salt'],correct:2,
    steps:['Write the ions: H⁺ + Cl⁻ and Na⁺ + OH⁻.','They swap partners: Na⁺ with Cl⁻, and H⁺ with OH⁻.','H⁺ + OH⁻ → H₂O is what makes it a neutralization. It releases heat, which is why the thermometer rises.']},
  {id:'handwarmer',kind:'real',lvl:'honors',
    q:'A disposable hand warmer heats up as soon as you open the packet. Why?',
    choices:['Iron powder inside reacts with oxygen from the air (rusting), and that synthesis reaction gives off heat','A battery inside warms it','Your hand warms it','The salt inside freezes'],correct:0,
    steps:['The packet holds iron powder, salt, water and carbon. Opening it lets air in.','Iron combines with oxygen: 4Fe + 3O₂ → 2Fe₂O₃, a synthesis reaction.','Rusting is exothermic. A nail rusts too slowly to feel; fine powder and salt make it fast enough to warm your hands for hours.']},
  {id:'redox',kind:'test',lvl:'ap',
    q:'Why is every single-replacement reaction a redox reaction, while a precipitation (double-replacement) reaction is not?',
    choices:['Single replacement always uses oxygen','In single replacement a free element becomes an ion (or the reverse), so electrons move; in double replacement the ions keep their charges and only swap partners','Double replacement happens in water','They are both redox'],correct:1,
    steps:['Zn + Cu²⁺ → Zn²⁺ + Cu: zinc goes from 0 to +2 (oxidized) and copper from +2 to 0 (reduced).','In AgNO₃ + NaCl → AgCl + NaNO₃, every ion has the same charge before and after.','No change in oxidation numbers means no electron transfer, so it is not redox.']}
]},
activity:{questions:[
  {id:'whowins',kind:'test',lvl:'bio',
    q:'Copper wire in silver nitrate grows silver crystals. Silver wire in copper nitrate does nothing. Why?',
    choices:['Silver is heavier','Copper wire is thicker','Nitrate only reacts with copper','Copper is more active: it gives up electrons more easily, so it can push silver out of solution, but silver can’t push copper out'],correct:3,
    steps:['A metal replaces another only if it is higher on the activity series.','Copper is above silver, so Cu gives electrons to Ag⁺: Cu + 2Ag⁺ → Cu²⁺ + 2Ag.','Silver is below copper, so it can’t give electrons to Cu²⁺. No reaction.']},
  {id:'acidfizz',kind:'test',lvl:'bio',
    q:'Zinc fizzes in hydrochloric acid, but copper does not. Why?',
    choices:['Zinc is above hydrogen in the activity series, so it replaces H⁺ and makes hydrogen gas; copper is below hydrogen','Copper is too shiny','Zinc is a gas','The acid is too weak for zinc'],correct:0,
    steps:['Hydrogen has a place in the activity series too.','Metals above hydrogen give electrons to H⁺: Zn + 2HCl → ZnCl₂ + H₂, and the H₂ is the fizz.','Copper is below hydrogen, so it can’t reduce H⁺, and nothing happens.']},
  {id:'boatzinc',kind:'real',lvl:'bio',
    q:'Boat owners bolt blocks of zinc to steel hulls and propeller shafts. Why?',
    choices:['To make the boat heavier','Zinc keeps barnacles away','Zinc is more active than iron, so the zinc corrodes instead of the steel','Zinc makes the boat faster'],correct:2,
    steps:['In salt water, metals touching each other act like a battery.','The more active metal gives up electrons and corrodes first. Zinc is above iron.','So the zinc block (a “sacrificial anode”) wears away and is replaced, while the steel stays protected.']},
  {id:'gold',kind:'real',lvl:'bio',
    q:'Gold coins from shipwrecks come up shiny after hundreds of years in salt water. Why?',
    choices:['Gold is painted','Gold is at the very bottom of the activity series: it almost never gives up electrons, so it doesn’t corrode','Salt water protects gold','Gold is too heavy to react'],correct:1,
    steps:['Corrosion is a metal losing electrons to something around it.','The lower a metal is on the series, the less it wants to give up electrons.','Gold is at the bottom, so it barely reacts, which is why it is used for jewelry and electrical contacts.']},
  {id:'bluefade2',kind:'test',lvl:'honors',
    q:'Zinc goes into blue Cu²⁺ solution. Why does the solution lose its blue color as the zinc turns red-brown?',
    choices:['Zinc dissolves the color','The water gets cooler','Copper turns into zinc','Cu²⁺ ions take electrons from zinc and become copper metal on the zinc; the Zn²⁺ ions left behind are colorless'],correct:3,
    steps:['Zinc is oxidized: Zn → Zn²⁺ + 2e⁻.','Copper ions are reduced: Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu, the red-brown coating.','Blue Cu²⁺ leaves the solution and colorless Zn²⁺ replaces it, so the blue fades.']},
  {id:'battery',kind:'real',lvl:'honors',
    q:'Why are very active metals like lithium and zinc used in batteries?',
    choices:['They are cheap','They are good insulators','They give up electrons easily, and those electrons are what flow through your device','They don’t react with anything'],correct:2,
    steps:['A battery is a redox reaction with the electrons sent through a wire.','The more active metal is oxidized and pushes electrons out.','The farther apart the two materials are on the series, the higher the voltage. Lithium is the most active, which is why lithium batteries pack so much energy.']},
  {id:'gap',kind:'test',lvl:'ap',
    q:'Why does a Zn/Cu cell give a higher voltage than a Fe/Cu cell?',
    choices:['The activity series is ordered by E°; zinc is farther below copper in E° than iron is, so E°cell = E°cathode − E°anode is larger','Zinc is heavier than iron','Iron cells always run at 1.5 V','Copper changes in each cell'],correct:0,
    steps:['E°(Cu²⁺/Cu) = +0.34 V, E°(Fe²⁺/Fe) = −0.44 V, E°(Zn²⁺/Zn) = −0.76 V.','Zn/Cu: 0.34 − (−0.76) = 1.10 V. Fe/Cu: 0.34 − (−0.44) = 0.78 V.','A bigger gap in the series means a bigger voltage.']}
]},
precip:{questions:[
  {id:'cloudy',kind:'test',lvl:'bio',
    q:'Clear silver nitrate and clear sodium chloride turn cloudy white when mixed. Why?',
    choices:['The solutions cool down','Air gets mixed in','Salt crystals reappear','The ions swap partners, and silver with chloride (AgCl) doesn’t dissolve in water, so it forms a solid'],correct:3,
    steps:['Each solution is ions floating in water: Ag⁺ + NO₃⁻, and Na⁺ + Cl⁻.','When they mix, Ag⁺ meets Cl⁻. AgCl is insoluble, so those ions lock together into tiny solid grains.','The grains scatter light, so you see a white cloud: a precipitate.']},
  {id:'nochange',kind:'test',lvl:'bio',
    q:'Potassium iodide and sodium chloride solutions are mixed. Nothing happens. Why?',
    choices:['The ions are too small','Every possible new pair (KCl and NaI) is soluble, so the ions just stay dissolved','Iodide never reacts','The solutions need to be heated'],correct:1,
    steps:['The ions could pair as K⁺ with Cl⁻ or Na⁺ with I⁻.','Both KCl and NaI dissolve in water.','With no insoluble pair, no solid forms. You just have four kinds of ions mixed together.']},
  {id:'scale',kind:'real',lvl:'bio',
    q:'In hard-water areas, kettles and shower heads build up a white crust. Why?',
    choices:['Hard water carries dissolved calcium; heating it forms calcium carbonate, which doesn’t dissolve, so it comes out as solid scale','Soap leaves it behind','The metal of the kettle flakes off','Chlorine from tap water dries out'],correct:0,
    steps:['Hard water has Ca²⁺ and Mg²⁺ ions dissolved from rock.','Heating drives off CO₂ and leaves carbonate ions, and Ca²⁺ + CO₃²⁻ → CaCO₃(s).','That precipitate is scale. Vinegar removes it because the acid dissolves calcium carbonate.']},
  {id:'alum',kind:'real',lvl:'bio',
    q:'Water treatment plants add alum (aluminum sulfate) to muddy river water. Why?',
    choices:['To make the water taste better','To kill bacteria','It forms a sticky solid (aluminum hydroxide) that traps dirt, so the clumps sink and can be filtered out','To soften the water'],correct:2,
    steps:['Alum dissolves into Al³⁺ ions.','In water, Al³⁺ forms Al(OH)₃, an insoluble, gel-like precipitate.','The fluffy solid grabs tiny particles of mud, the clumps settle, and the clearer water moves on to filters.']},
  {id:'spectator',kind:'test',lvl:'honors',
    q:'Why are Na⁺ and NO₃⁻ left out of the net ionic equation for AgNO₃ + NaCl → AgCl + NaNO₃?',
    choices:['They are too small to matter','They are spectator ions: dissolved before and still dissolved after, so they don’t take part','They react with water','They form a gas'],correct:1,
    steps:['Write every soluble compound as ions: Ag⁺ + NO₃⁻ + Na⁺ + Cl⁻ → AgCl(s) + Na⁺ + NO₃⁻.','Na⁺ and NO₃⁻ appear unchanged on both sides.','Cross them out to get the change that actually happens: Ag⁺ + Cl⁻ → AgCl(s).']},
  {id:'bluesolid',kind:'test',lvl:'honors',
    q:'Mixing copper(II) sulfate with sodium hydroxide makes a pale blue solid. Why?',
    choices:['Sodium sulfate is blue','The NaOH turns blue in light','Sulfate always makes solids','Cu²⁺ and OH⁻ form copper(II) hydroxide, which is insoluble; copper gives it the blue color'],correct:3,
    steps:['Possible new pairs: Cu²⁺ with OH⁻, and Na⁺ with SO₄²⁻.','Na₂SO₄ is soluble, but Cu(OH)₂ is not.','Net ionic: Cu²⁺ + 2OH⁻ → Cu(OH)₂(s). The copper ion is what gives the solid its blue color.']},
  {id:'limiting',kind:'test',lvl:'ap',
    q:'25.0 mL of 0.100 M AgNO₃ is mixed with 15.0 mL of 0.100 M NaCl. How many moles of AgCl form, and why?',
    choices:['2.50 × 10⁻³ mol, because Ag⁺ is in excess','4.00 × 10⁻³ mol, adding both','1.50 × 10⁻³ mol, because Cl⁻ runs out first','1.00 × 10⁻³ mol, the difference'],correct:2,
    steps:['Moles of Ag⁺ = 0.0250 L × 0.100 M = 2.50 × 10⁻³ mol. Moles of Cl⁻ = 0.0150 L × 0.100 M = 1.50 × 10⁻³ mol.','Ag⁺ + Cl⁻ → AgCl is 1:1, so Cl⁻ is limiting.','1.50 × 10⁻³ mol AgCl forms (about 0.215 g), and 1.00 × 10⁻³ mol Ag⁺ stays in solution.']}
]}
});
